+## Surfaces
+
+### Terminal panes stay PTYs; thread panes never wrap one
+
+A terminal pane is the agent CLI on a real PTY, rendered by libghostty-vt, with input
+delivered unchanged. A thread pane runs the same provider through its structured protocol
+(Agent SDK, Codex app-server, ACP or the provider's SDK server) and renders typed events.
+A thread pane never reads, parses or re-renders terminal output, and no terminal pane is
+replaced by a chat view. Reopening a conversation in the other surface resumes the
+provider's session by exact id; one conversation is never open in both surfaces at once.
+
+**Why.** Terminal fidelity is what makes a pane trustworthy: every key reaches the CLI and
+every byte the CLI writes is shown. Mixing the two would make neither surface faithful.
+**Enforced by** review.
+
+### Thread traffic never shares the terminal byte path
+
+Thread events travel as typed control messages, coalesced per pane and sent only to
+clients that show the pane. They never pass through `frame_queue`, `vt.rs` or the
+scrollback ring. Text deltas are persisted when an item completes, not per token.
+
+**Why.** The frame queue and scrollback ring are sized for terminal bytes (4 MiB each) and
+the frame queue may drop bytes under pressure. A terminal recovers by repainting; a thread
+cannot recover a lost event, and a talkative agent must not evict terminal history.
+**Enforced by** `perf_smoke`: terminal throughput and latency with threads streaming stay
+within the noise of the terminal-only baseline.
+
+### Houston never decides a permission request
+
+Houston carries a decision; it never makes one. In a terminal pane the CLI's own prompt
+takes the answer and Houston only reports `NeedsInput`. In a thread pane the CLI hands the
+request to Houston, which renders it and sends exactly the decision someone made. Each
+request is keyed by the provider's request id and answered once; a stale or duplicate
+answer is refused. No timer, default or Houston policy answers on anyone's behalf.
+
+A parent orchestrator's authority is asymmetric. It may approve or deny routine requests
+from a child spawned with `--ask`. When a child's auto mode blocks an action as dangerous,
+the parent may deny it or escalate it with a recommendation, but only the user may
+approve it, in either surface.
+
+**Why.** An approval is consent. An answer nobody chose would approve actions the user
+never saw, and an agent approving what the CLI's safety check refused would turn that
+check into a suggestion. **Enforced by** review.
+
